  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Origin and Mechanism
    
    Thaw-Kept is an emergency statute enacted by the Aurora Stack Council during the Frozen Peace, suspending the colony's longstanding requirement that governance decisions be taken only under aurora-blessed skies. The law permits a single **Luminary**, selected by lottery during the first weeks of the Darkening — the seventeen-day aurora failure that paralyzed Promenade Hei — to act without consensus when the aurora clouds or when threat-runners report orca movement.
    
    The mechanism is straightforward: one penguin, chosen by chance rather than rank, receives authority that otherwise requires the entire Council under clear skies.
    
    ## Persistence
    
    Thaw-Kept was drafted as temporary. It has not been revoked. The reasons are cumulative rather than singular. Each subsequent crisis — the Breach, the collapse Of The First Shelf, the near-failure of the Gilding trade festival, the fragile signing of the Accord — added another layer of justification. Revocation would require acknowledging that some decisions made under its authority were mistakes, and no Council has found that acknowledgment politically survivable.
    
    The law's most cited defense concerns Grandfather Blood, the orca war-leader whose final raid on the Stack's eastern spires was repelled by a Luminary acting under Thaw-Kept authority. That Luminary died in the counterattack. Emergency powers sealed in someone's death acquire a permanence no committee can undo.
    
    ## Section Twelve and the Tribunals
    
    Section Twelve — emergency jurisdiction over external threats to colony integrity — has been repurposed for a use its drafters never anticipated: convening cross-cultural tribunals that try orcas under Aurora Stack procedural law. In the tribunal of Luminary Xuzhong Pelm'Oath, which tried the orcas Ruibung and Grandfather Blood for trespass at the sealed Drowned Ledge, the subsection a Luminary chose to cite determined whether orca testimony was admissible. The law written to repel raids now governs sentencing. Whether this represents institutional flexibility or institutional drift depends on which subsection one reads.
